Third-party source: mickys/nvenc, https://github.com/mickys/nvenc
Commit 1e91845d6f37d8bce671d62c14f4390324da3659 (release 0.9.5), MIT licence (LICENSE, Copyright (c) 2026 mickys).
Imported: src/ and vt/ and LICENSE. Left out: the upstream tools, trace interposers, test streams, benchmark data and
documentation (about 100 MB), none of which the library needs.

Changes made here, in this order of importance:
  vt/nvencvt.c   NVEncVT_RegisterDecoders(): registers only the decoders, for the decode service (appended before
                 load_conf).
                 EnableLowLatencyRateControl: declared as a selection property of the H.264 and HEVC encoders, accepted by
                 SetProperty / CopyProperty and listed among the supported properties. A session that sets it is real-time
                 with no B pictures. Without the declaration VideoToolbox drops the encoder from the candidates of any
                 client that asks for low-latency rate control (FFmpeg's low_delay, Sunshine), and answers -12902.

  src/hevcparse.c, src/h264parse.c, src/av1parse.c  Fixes found by running the parsers under AddressSanitizer with a coverage-guided
                 fuzzer (tools/test_nvenc_parsers.py holds one case for each; to be sent upstream):
                 - ue() clamps to 2^28: a code up to 2^32 - 2 became a negative int, passed "id > max" checks and indexed the SPS / PPS
                   arrays (an out-of-bounds write when the parameter set was stored);
                 - scaling_list_data(): pred_matrix_id_delta checked against 0..matrixId before it indexes the list array;
                 - st_ref_pic_set(): the inter-predicted lists were written past 32 entries before the limit was checked;
                 - hrd_parameters(): cpb_cnt_minus1 limited to 31 (a stream that had ended still ran 2^28 loop rounds);
                 - hevc_parse_sps() / h264_parse_sps(): the sizes, bit depths and block sizes of the specification's ranges are
                   checked before the SPS is stored (16 * width overflowed int); av1 decode_unsigned_subexp_with_ref() shifts unsigned.

  Blackwell and the other Turing chips (tools/test_nvencvt_blackwell.py), written from public sources only:
                 - src/rm.c: Blackwell's classes 0xcfb7, 0xcfb0, 0xc96f (open-gpu-kernel-modules clcfb7.h, clcfb0.h, clc96f.h);
                 - src/blocklinear.c: gob_offset / gob_untile_plane / gob_layout_for, the Blackwell8Bit and Blackwell16Bit plane
                   layouts and the rule that picks one by element size (Mesa NIL, src/nouveau/nil/tiling.rs and copy.rs, MIT);
                 - src/dec.c: the H.264 decoder untiles in the card's layout;
                 - src/channel.c: methods that do not fit the push buffer are dropped and the submission fails (it wrote past
                   the mapping when a channel stopped fetching); the buffer is reused after a completion the decoder saw,
                   because clc96f.h documents no GPGet word;
                 - vt/: card kinds for Blackwell (H.264 decoder only) and TU10x / TU116 (decoders only), by PCI device ID.
                 Not run on a card yet. Nothing registers on these cards unless NVMTL_NVENCVT names the process or is 1.

  VP9, AV1 and HEVC decode on Blackwell, by the rtx5060ti fork (its changes here are MIT, as the library; Copyright (c)
  2026 kevineduardo) (tools/test_nvencvt_blackwell.py), checked bit-exact on an RTX 5060 Ti against FFmpeg's
  software decoders (libvpx and AV1 conformance vectors and generated streams: 8- and 10-bit, inter frames, 4x4 tiles,
  error resilient, SVC, 4K):
                 - src/nvdec_blackwell.h: method 0x4dc (a buffer the engine needs for inter pictures; without it every
                   inter picture ends in a channel error) and the picture setup's Blackwell fields (the planes' GOB kinds
                   and surface format code, which make the engine write the Blackwell plane layouts its inter prediction
                   reads; VP9 +0x108 / AV1 +0x244 units word; AV1 reserved12 = 0xffff). Sources: the methods NullMoth's own
                   HEVC decoder sends on that card (captured from its push buffer), and the values the AROS nvgfx driver
                   and cagedbird043/nvrm record from NVIDIA's driver on class CFB0 (facts of the interface, no code taken);
                 - src/vp9dec.c, src/av1dec.c: planes read, edge-extended and migrated in the card's layout (gob_*);
                 - vt/nvencvt_dec.c: VP9 and AV1 register on Blackwell; HEVC and MPEG stay off there.
                 - src/vp9dec.c, src/av1dec.c: chan_done() after each completion, as src/dec.c: Blackwell's channel class
                   reports no GPGet, so the 64 KB push buffer was never reused and decoding stopped after ~200 pictures;
                 - src/blocklinear.c: gob_untile_plane copies whole Blackwell tiles without per-run offset arithmetic
                   (4K 8-bit AV1: 5.2 -> 3.4 ms per picture), checked sample by sample against gob_offset.
                 - src/channel.c: GPPut read back before the doorbell (USERD is write-combined video memory; the host now
                   and then fetched the old GPPut and never ran the picture: 7 of 9600 4K AV1 pictures under AVFoundation,
                   0 with the read), chan_wait() rings the doorbell again for a picture 50 ms late (dec.c, vp9dec.c,
                   av1dec.c), and the push buffer wraps once half used instead of after every picture;
                 - 10-bit VP9 / AV1 for a client that asks for 8-bit NV12 (AVAssetReader, transcoders): narrowed while
                   untiled (gob_untile_plane_narrow, (v + 0x80) >> 8), not converted by VideoToolbox afterwards
                   (4K: 16 -> 93 fps; identical to the software decode rounded the same way). NVDEC_NONARROW=1: as before.
                 - src/hevcdec.c: HEVC on Blackwell with the same additions (HEVC's setup places: reserved6, reserved7,
                   +0x11c, +0x120), planes in the card's layout, chan_wait / chan_done, 8-bit narrowing; registered on
                   Blackwell above NullMoth's closed HEVC decoder (bit-exact on 17 JCT-VC conformance streams; faster
                   through ffmpeg and for 8-bit clients, slower at 4K 8-bit through AVFoundation; see the doc).
                 - vt/nvdec_pace.h (new), vt/nvencvt_dec.c: in real-time playback (kVTDecodeFrame_1xRealTimePlayback) an
                   HEVC stream the decoder cannot keep up with is decoded to the temporal sub-layers it can afford,
                   instead of the player dropping pictures in bursts (Apple's 4K 240 fps aerials: 27 pictures a second in
                   steps of 2 and 16 became 62 in steps of 4); the client's pixel format is found in a list of formats
                   (it was taken for another format and VideoToolbox converted every picture);
                   kVTDecodeFrame_DoNotOutputFrame pictures are not copied out. tools/test_nvdec_pace.py.
                 - src/blocklinear.c: the plane copies run on two threads (one each while several sessions decode)
                   instead of one block per band on every core: the same time, a tenth of the CPU.

Where upstream's own facts come from (read before calling this library clean of NVIDIA's closed driver): the decoders'
picture setups are open-gpu-doc's nvdec_drv.h. The ENCODERS' per-picture control blocks (src/h264enctmpl.h,
hevcenctmpl.h, av1enctmpl.h) are byte tables that upstream generated from traces of NVIDIA's Linux driver encoding on
the same cards, and src/enc.c, la.h and two decoder comments cite such traces for buffer sizes and method use; the
input layout in blocklinear.h was measured from a working client's surface. That is observation of a driver's output,
not disassembly, but it is the same kind of source as NullMoth's own encoder libraries.

